<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yarlung Tsangpo</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Yarlung Zangbo</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Yarlung Tsangpo is the upper course of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmaputra">Brahmaputra</a>, flowing eastwards across southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> for more than 1,000 km before cutting through the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himalayas">Himalaya</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Its valley and those of its tributaries, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhasa">Lhasa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shigatse">Shigatse</a>, have long been the agricultural and political heartland of Tibet, and the Yarlung kings who founded the Tibetan Empire rose in a side valley south of the river.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yarlung-tsangpo.jpg" alt="Yarlung Tsangpo" width="320" height="207" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Boqiang Liao from Athens,Ohio, US · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brahmaputra_River,_Shigatse.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Yarlung Tsangpo, also called Yarlung Zangbo (Chinese Yalu Zangbu Jiang), is the upper course of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmaputra">Brahmaputra</a>. It flows through the Tibet Autonomous Region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arunachal-pradesh">Arunachal Pradesh</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, and it is the longest river of Tibet. Its upper section is also called Dangque Zangbu, meaning Horse River. It rises at the Angsi Glacier in western Tibet, southeast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-kailash">Mount Kailash</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-manasarovar">Lake Manasarovar</a>, and then forms the South Tibet Valley, which is about 1,200 kilometres long and 300 kilometres wide. The valley descends from 4,500 metres to 3,000 metres above sea level, and the vegetation changes from cold desert to arid steppe, deciduous scrub, and finally conifer and rhododendron forest.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The river's basin lies between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himalayas">Himalayas</a> in the south and the Kang Rinpoche and Nyenchen Tanglha mountains in the north. Its climate is less severe than that of the higher parts of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>, and the basin is home to most of the population of the Tibet Autonomous Region. Where the river leaves the Tibetan Plateau it bends around Namcha Barwa in a horseshoe and forms the Yarlung Tsangpo Grand Canyon, the deepest and possibly the longest canyon in the world. It then enters <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arunachal-pradesh">Arunachal Pradesh</a>, where it widens and is called the Siang, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a> it becomes the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmaputra">Brahmaputra</a>.</p><p>The river has three major waterfalls. The largest, the Hidden Falls, became known in the West in 1998, when its sighting was briefly hailed as a discovery, although Tibetan hunters and Buddhist monks had told stories of the great falls. Chinese authorities replied that Chinese geographers, exploring the gorge since 1973, had already photographed the falls from a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helicopter">helicopter</a> in 1987. Since the 1990s kayaking teams have attempted the river. A Japanese group lost a member in 1993, and the National Geographic Society's 1998 expedition ended with the death of the kayaker Doug Gordon. In January and February 2002 an international group made the first descent of the upper gorge.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The main tributaries are the Nyangchu, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhasa">Lhasa</a>, the Nyang and the Parlung Tsangpo; the Nyang is the longest. In November 2020 the chairman of PowerChina announced the start of a hydroelectric project on the river. In December 2024 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> approved the construction of the Medog Hydropower Station, intended as the largest hydropower dam in the world, at a cost of about 1 trillion yuan. Construction began on 19 July 2025. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangladesh">Bangladesh</a> raised concerns about its effects in their countries.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yarlung Tsangpo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Tibetan Empire grew out of the Yarlung valley in south-central Tibet, where a line of chiefs later remembered as the first kings of Tibet held lands south of the <b>Yarlung Tsangpo</b> river.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tibetan-empire">Tibetan Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
